WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:03.419
I get this initial joy that I'm very happy about having proved it,

00:00:03.419 --> 00:00:08.188
then I also get this fear that I'm about to completely embarrass myself in front of the mathematical community

00:00:08.530 --> 00:00:14.520
by claiming to have proved some well-known result when actually there'll be some obvious mistake because I've, I

00:00:14.980 --> 00:00:16.930
put a plus instead of a minus somewhere.

00:00:16.930 --> 00:00:20.579
So you get this euphoric joy and adrenaline rush,

00:00:20.580 --> 00:00:26.699
but then it's followed pretty quickly by complete fear that I'm about to trash my reputation and do something completely idiotic.

00:00:27.940 --> 00:00:33.760
Today I'm going to talk about a conjecture of Duffin and Schaeffer that was recently proven by myself and my

00:00:33.840 --> 00:00:35.840
collaborator Dimitris Koukoulopoulos.

00:00:35.920 --> 00:00:38.760
(Brady: So this is like, math news, is it?)
- Yeah exactly,

00:00:38.820 --> 00:00:42.480
hot off the press. So this is a problem about Diophantine approximation.

00:00:42.730 --> 00:00:46.950
Let's just take a step back and think about what Diophantine approximation is, in general.

00:00:47.050 --> 00:00:52.500
So maybe the most simple numbers we know are just the whole numbers: 1, 2,

00:00:53.020 --> 00:00:58.349
3, 4, and so on.  So slightly more complicated, there's rational numbers;

00:00:58.349 --> 00:01:02.660
which is just ratios or fractions of whole numbers. So,

00:01:03.300 --> 00:01:05.140
one half, one-third

00:01:05.140 --> 00:01:08.790
two-thirds. But as well as rational numbers, there's irrational numbers

00:01:08.980 --> 00:01:13.280
which can't be expressed as just a ratio of two whole numbers.

00:01:13.280 --> 00:01:16.960
Euler's number e starts off as

00:01:17.640 --> 00:01:19.640
2.71828

00:01:20.229 --> 00:01:23.368
and then goes on.  
- (Brady: How far do you know that one?)

00:01:24.070 --> 00:01:29.459
Ok, I know it up to 2 8, and then I stopped. So it's no coincidence where I draw the dots.

00:01:30.579 --> 00:01:33.839
Similarly, pi is another famous irrational number

00:01:33.840 --> 00:01:38.000
3.14159

00:01:38.060 --> 00:01:44.180
And these are numbers where we've proven that you can't write it as just a ratio of two whole numbers,

00:01:44.290 --> 00:01:47.579
and in fact, there's a whole zoo of numbers out there

00:01:47.579 --> 00:01:52.259
and in many ways most numbers can't be written as just a ratio of two whole numbers.

00:01:52.259 --> 00:01:55.769
So most numbers are these complicated numbers that seem to have some

00:01:56.200 --> 00:02:01.619
crazy decimal expansion that probably has no patterns in it whatsoever. And so then there's a question as to

00:02:02.259 --> 00:02:04.589
what do we even really mean by these irrational numbers?

00:02:04.590 --> 00:02:06.659
So I put these dot dot dots here,

00:02:07.119 --> 00:02:13.409
really what this expression is saying is: I have lots of different approximations to the number pi,

00:02:14.020 --> 00:02:16.889
which get more and more accurate.  So maybe a first

00:02:17.530 --> 00:02:19.480
approximation is that

00:02:19.480 --> 00:02:27.060
pi is roughly equal to three, and then a second approximation is pi is approximately equal to three point one, which is

00:02:27.640 --> 00:02:33.959
31 over ten, and then a better approximation is that pi is approximately equal to

00:02:34.900 --> 00:02:41.670
314 over 100,  3.14 and so on. This is really what we mean by an irrational number;

00:02:41.910 --> 00:02:45.749
we can't just uniquely define it in a very easy way typically,

00:02:45.880 --> 00:02:49.109
but we can define it by the sequence of simple approximations.

00:02:49.239 --> 00:02:55.349
The sequence gets closer and closer in on one number - pi for example was historically very important for engineers to

00:02:55.989 --> 00:02:57.670
do certain bits of architecture,

00:02:57.670 --> 00:03:02.280
and they needed to know it to a certain level of precision. And to a given number position

00:03:02.280 --> 00:03:08.009
you can just approximate it by some rational number. 
- (Brady: Good enough to build a bridge or something?)
- Yeah, exactly

00:03:08.260 --> 00:03:13.800
Every irrational number has a decimal expansion like this and that's one way of understanding them, but

00:03:14.290 --> 00:03:18.480
sometimes though, rather more efficient ways of writing them as a ratio

00:03:18.640 --> 00:03:24.029
rather than just numbers with denominators 10 or 100 or 1000 or so. So one famous one

00:03:24.029 --> 00:03:29.760
is that pi is approximately equal to 22/7.

00:03:29.920 --> 00:03:34.540
And even though maybe 22 over 7 is a much simpler looking fraction than

00:03:35.049 --> 00:03:41.279
314 over 100, because the numbers are quite a lot smaller, pi is actually closer to 22 over 7 that it is to

00:03:42.220 --> 00:03:46.799
314 over 100. And so this is somehow a very efficient way of approximating pi and

00:03:47.620 --> 00:03:50.730
so if you an engineer or something you wouldn't have to care about

00:03:51.220 --> 00:03:54.269
314 over 100, you could just use 22 over 7 and that'd work much better.

00:03:54.549 --> 00:03:59.939
It's a very famous theorem of Dirichlet that every rational number has a

00:04:00.519 --> 00:04:03.089
fairly efficient sequence of approximations,

00:04:03.730 --> 00:04:08.640
like 22 over 7, that approximate the number better than you might expect.

00:04:08.640 --> 00:04:14.399
(Brady: So is there like a mathematical name for them, these like - these silver bullets?)
- For this sort of setup, they're known as the

00:04:14.980 --> 00:04:16.980
convergents to the irrational number.

00:04:17.520 --> 00:04:21.359
There's an algorithm for producing - if you give me your favourite irrational number

00:04:21.360 --> 00:04:26.819
I can do some calculations and it can spit out these convergents, which are these very efficient approximations to the number

00:04:26.820 --> 00:04:31.290
Dirichlet's Theorem says that regardless of what irrational number you choose,

00:04:31.660 --> 00:04:35.910
let's say your favourite irrational number is some number alpha, then you can find

00:04:36.490 --> 00:04:39.180
lots of different approximations of rationals,

00:04:39.730 --> 00:04:41.730
let's call it a over q, and

00:04:42.460 --> 00:04:47.039
this is going to be an efficient approximation in the sense that the error term,

00:04:47.530 --> 00:04:50.700
in the approximation, is at most 1 over q squared.

00:04:51.310 --> 00:04:53.970
So regardless of what irrational number you choose

00:04:54.610 --> 00:04:55.660
there's

00:04:55.660 --> 00:04:59.730
infinitely many different pairs (a,q) where you have this efficient approximation

00:05:00.040 --> 00:05:05.280
where the error is pretty close.  By comparison, when we just doing a decimal expansion,

00:05:05.350 --> 00:05:09.360
we would just have an error term which looks like 1 over the denominator.

00:05:09.360 --> 00:05:14.249
And so it's not anywhere near as efficient. The whole problem of Diophantine approximation is

00:05:14.890 --> 00:05:20.789
variations on this question: if you give me an irrational number how well can I approximate it by

00:05:21.220 --> 00:05:28.830
rational numbers, and how efficiently can I approximate it? But unfortunately this whole area is a zoo of different

00:05:29.260 --> 00:05:30.750
complicated impossible questions.

00:05:30.750 --> 00:05:36.360
We know from Dirichlet's theorem that we can approximate pi with an error 1 over q squared.

00:05:36.610 --> 00:05:40.139
So one natural question is, when I do even more efficient approximations,

00:05:40.180 --> 00:05:45.060
can I get maybe an approximation that looks like one of q cubed?  So that'd be even better, and

00:05:46.480 --> 00:05:48.240
this is a well-known open problem.

00:05:48.240 --> 00:05:51.900
We don't know if you can approximate pi to that level of efficiency.

00:05:52.330 --> 00:05:57.270
(Brady: Has until now 1 over q squared been the best you could do?)
- Yeah for pi,

00:05:57.270 --> 00:06:02.490
I don't think we really know anything better than 1 over q squared.  For some special irrational numbers

00:06:02.530 --> 00:06:04.919
you can do better than 1 over q squared and

00:06:05.500 --> 00:06:09.630
to really show you how pathetic we are and how little we know about these problems

00:06:10.000 --> 00:06:16.140
you could consider the number e plus pi, and we don't even know if e plus pi is

00:06:16.750 --> 00:06:18.250
rational or not.

00:06:18.250 --> 00:06:22.590
So it could be that e plus pi is equal to just

00:06:22.990 --> 00:06:28.199
a over q, for some whole numbers a and q. It's clearly not going to be the case,

00:06:28.210 --> 00:06:33.569
but as mathematicians we have no way of disproving this.
- (Brady: That'd be amazing if someone found that!)
- Yeah,

00:06:33.570 --> 00:06:36.900
so it would be completely crazy and you can check small numbers,

00:06:36.900 --> 00:06:39.449
so it's got to be the case that a and q would have to -

00:06:39.670 --> 00:06:42.720
you can prove that they'd have to have loads and loads of digits if they

00:06:42.850 --> 00:06:47.040
possibly were this, and kind of it clearly the case that this can't be the case.

00:06:47.040 --> 00:06:48.840
But we have no way of proving this at all.

00:06:48.840 --> 00:06:55.769
And this is really typical for these Diophantine approximation problems: that they're really complicated problems and subtle problems,

00:06:55.960 --> 00:07:01.979
that we really don't understand very well at all as mathematicians. And there are some numbers that can be approximated very well

00:07:02.530 --> 00:07:05.970
but almost all numbers you can't really do much better than one over q squared.

00:07:05.970 --> 00:07:08.999
You can do a tiny bit better than Dirichlet's theorem. But Dirichlet's theorem

00:07:09.000 --> 00:07:10.860
is almost as good you can do.

00:07:10.940 --> 00:07:16.220
Duffin and Schaeffer asked a huge generalisation of this question where you are only allowed to use specific

00:07:16.240 --> 00:07:20.920
denominators q. So maybe you choose the primes, or the squares, or

00:07:21.730 --> 00:07:23.730
whatever crazy subset of

00:07:23.770 --> 00:07:30.090
the integers that you like. And so, the question that Duffin and Schaeffer asked was: if you choose your favourite sequence of

00:07:30.400 --> 00:07:35.220
rationals, how well can you approximate these numbers? What's the efficiency of the approximations?

00:07:35.440 --> 00:07:40.650
It was very much part of their philosophy that they wanted a super general theorem

00:07:40.650 --> 00:07:46.769
that would work for absolutely every possible choice of denominators. So at first sight this seems

00:07:47.680 --> 00:07:52.859
crazily ambitious, that I've already said there's these basic problems like e plus pi, and we

00:07:53.260 --> 00:07:57.360
don't even know whether they're rational or not. But then suddenly we're asking for

00:07:57.970 --> 00:08:02.999
arbitrary approximations, of almost arbitrary quality, for arbitrary sets of integers.

00:08:03.000 --> 00:08:07.110
So it seems like this crazy big generalisation, and we know already that

00:08:07.390 --> 00:08:10.650
you're going to have to allow for some small number of exceptions.

00:08:10.780 --> 00:08:12.400
But then there's an

00:08:12.400 --> 00:08:19.919
amazing theorem of Cassels and then also Gallagher, who showed that you choose your set of denominators, and you choose

00:08:20.110 --> 00:08:23.460
what threshold you have for efficiency, and

00:08:24.070 --> 00:08:26.220
regardless of how you set it up; either

00:08:26.920 --> 00:08:34.319
almost all numbers can be approximated using those denominators at that level of efficiency, or almost no numbers can be.

00:08:34.660 --> 00:08:36.660
So you have this crazy

00:08:36.820 --> 00:08:40.170
simplicity suddenly in this whole world of complex problems

00:08:40.570 --> 00:08:47.699
that either almost everything can be approximated in the way that you want, or almost nothing can be approximated in the way that you want

00:08:48.310 --> 00:08:51.419
regardless of how you choose your denominators or how you choose your

00:08:52.149 --> 00:08:57.119
efficiency of approximations. So we know that you get either everything or virtually nothing

00:08:57.519 --> 00:09:00.119
but then the question is: which one of the two cases are we in?

00:09:01.149 --> 00:09:09.029
And so the Duffin-Schaeffer conjecture was saying there should be a simple criterion as to whether you get almost everything or almost

00:09:09.519 --> 00:09:13.499
nothing. So we can write this down. You choose your denominators

00:09:13.500 --> 00:09:19.949
so let's call them q_1, q_2, and so on, and then you choose the

00:09:20.560 --> 00:09:24.899
tolerable errors, so how efficiently you want to do it, for each denominator.

00:09:24.960 --> 00:09:29.800
So let's call it E_1, E_2, and so on. And then you want to know:

00:09:30.000 --> 00:09:34.940
can you approximate your irrational number alpha by

00:09:35.680 --> 00:09:40.320
denominators from your favourite set, with the error term of your choice of error terms?

00:09:40.389 --> 00:09:42.659
This is very difficult for an individual alpha

00:09:42.760 --> 00:09:49.019
but we know that either you can approximate almost all of them or almost none of them and then the Duffin-Schafffer conjecture,

00:09:49.120 --> 00:09:54.239
now a theorem, is that there's a simple test for whether you can get almost all of them

00:09:54.240 --> 00:09:58.199
or almost none of them.  
- (Brady: Is there an example - is this something you can't apply an example to?)

00:09:58.199 --> 00:10:03.749
So this is the slightly frustrating thing about the theorem, that because it's so general and you always allow an exception,

00:10:04.839 --> 00:10:08.219
set, you can't say for any individual irrational number

00:10:08.620 --> 00:10:14.849
whether you can approximate things. But one possible example is you could choose your denominators

00:10:15.730 --> 00:10:19.260
being the squares. So 1, 4, 9,

00:10:19.959 --> 00:10:21.160
16,

00:10:21.160 --> 00:10:28.050
and so on. And I now have to think about what the error terms might be. One choice is I could choose

00:10:28.300 --> 00:10:32.190
one over the denominator. So these are gradually getting better and better approximations.

00:10:32.560 --> 00:10:36.659
So this would be 1 over 1, 1 over 4, 1 over 9

00:10:37.060 --> 00:10:43.500
1 over 16, and so on. And it's not too difficult to see with these acceptable errors that you can

00:10:43.779 --> 00:10:50.039
approximate basically every irrational with these error terms. So this is not very ambitious.

00:10:50.380 --> 00:10:55.800
Maybe a more ambitious thing would be to try and do what we did here and have one over the denominator squared.

00:10:55.920 --> 00:10:59.680
We're looking at smaller error terms and so it's more accurate approximations.

00:11:00.220 --> 00:11:02.740
I could be more ambitious and try and do the same as Dirichlet's Theorem

00:11:02.760 --> 00:11:05.520
but where the denominators are just the squares, so

00:11:06.190 --> 00:11:10.230
the outer terms would be 1/1, 1/4 squared

00:11:10.870 --> 00:11:12.870
1 over 9 squared,

00:11:13.420 --> 00:11:14.950
and so on.

00:11:14.950 --> 00:11:15.960
And then again

00:11:15.960 --> 00:11:19.650
there's a simple test as to whether you can approximate almost all numbers,

00:11:19.840 --> 00:11:27.059
or almost no numbers and in this case the Duffin-Schaffer conjecture, now a theorem, says you were being too ambitious here

00:11:27.060 --> 00:11:33.840
and so actually almost no numbers can possibly be approximated at this level of accuracy with this choices of denominators.

00:11:33.940 --> 00:11:40.020
So you plug in your denominators, you plug in your error terms, and then there's a

00:11:40.420 --> 00:11:46.769
relatively simple thing that will say either: Yes, you can approximate virtually everything, or no you get basically nothing at all

00:11:46.770 --> 00:11:49.650
(Brady: Could I give you just any arbitrary collection of)

00:11:50.290 --> 00:11:56.040
(denominators? Could I just say like, the Brady numbers - here are the Brady numbers, will it work for pi and e and that?)

00:11:56.040 --> 00:11:58.439
(And you'll be able to plug it into your machine and say sorry - )

00:11:58.990 --> 00:12:04.620
(or, yep, it's gonna work.)
- Yeah, exactly. So again, there's always got to be a small number of exceptions because there's the crazy

00:12:05.620 --> 00:12:06.850
examples.

00:12:06.850 --> 00:12:09.659
But yeah, you choose your favourite Brady numbers and

00:12:10.390 --> 00:12:15.900
you say how will you want to approximate numbers by Brady numbers? And then there's a very simple test; either

00:12:16.270 --> 00:12:21.509
yeah, you can basically approximate everything or woah, woah, woah, you've been too ambitious,

00:12:21.780 --> 00:12:25.559
you can basically not approximate anything at all. And there's no in-between ground,

00:12:25.560 --> 00:12:29.489
it's not like you can approximate 50% of numbers or 30% of numbers;

00:12:29.560 --> 00:12:34.380
it's either 100% of numbers or 0% of numbers, which is kind of the beauty in this, that

00:12:35.050 --> 00:12:37.050
normally these problems are all so complicated

00:12:37.360 --> 00:12:42.510
but then there's this one moment where either you're getting virtually everything or virtually nothing and

00:12:42.670 --> 00:12:49.619
there's a very simple test now to tell which one of the two cases you're in.  That was what was really conjectured by Duffin and Schaeffer, they proposed

00:12:49.620 --> 00:12:53.340
the test. If you're in the field and you understand these things

00:12:53.340 --> 00:12:59.249
it's the most natural test you could possibly have but the problem was always, is this you need the true test?

00:12:59.470 --> 00:13:05.999
That we could prove that the test worked for lots of very special sequences of denominators and things,

00:13:06.040 --> 00:13:11.679
so if you chose the primes then we knew that it would work - the Duffin--Schaeffer test.

00:13:11.680 --> 00:13:14.650
But we didn't know for completely arbitrary

00:13:15.560 --> 00:13:16.750
choices of numbers, you know,

00:13:16.750 --> 00:13:19.299
the Brady numbers - you could have chosen them in such a way that

00:13:19.400 --> 00:13:24.970
we wouldn't know whether this was really the right test or not or if there's a subtle other thing that meant that the Duffin--Schaeffer

00:13:24.970 --> 00:13:29.289
test wasn't really the right criterion for whether you would get almost all numbers or almost none.

00:13:29.710 --> 00:13:36.549
(Brady: Are there certain types of denominators that work better and are more likely to give me that positive result,)

00:13:36.890 --> 00:13:39.729
(than other denominators? Like you said for primes)

00:13:40.250 --> 00:13:47.200
(usually it's going to be pretty good. Are there certain numbers that are more conducive to this than others?)
- Yeah, so there can be problems

00:13:47.720 --> 00:13:48.740
when

00:13:48.740 --> 00:13:51.880
the - your denominators have lots of small prime factors.

00:13:51.880 --> 00:13:54.729
And so this was really the

00:13:55.040 --> 00:13:57.340
big point behind Duffin and Schaeffer's work,

00:13:57.440 --> 00:14:02.770
that they realised that there was this subtlety that if you have lots of prime factors in your denominators

00:14:02.770 --> 00:14:09.009
you can write like the same rational number in lots of different ways. We have that the rational number 1/3 is equal

00:14:09.680 --> 00:14:17.470
to 2/6 and is equal to 3 ninths; and you can keep on writing these different rationals as being the same thing

00:14:17.750 --> 00:14:22.599
because you can cancel out a common factor. The only way that things wouldn't behave

00:14:23.180 --> 00:14:25.180
in the most obvious way

00:14:25.940 --> 00:14:27.080
would be

00:14:27.080 --> 00:14:29.619
if you kept on hitting the same numbers again,

00:14:29.620 --> 00:14:35.739
and then again, again; for precisely this reason that the numbers which are well approximated by one third are also

00:14:35.930 --> 00:14:39.940
well approximated by numbers with the denominator 6, and are also

00:14:40.310 --> 00:14:45.520
well approximated by numbers with denominator 9, because they're all basically the same numbers.

00:14:46.190 --> 00:14:52.299
(Brady: James, for this theorem to hold do the denominators have to have been produced in some kind of)

00:14:52.970 --> 00:14:54.380
(systematic)

00:14:54.380 --> 00:15:00.489
(algorithmic way? Could I have just pulled 50 numbers out of, you know, a bucket and said these are the denominators?)

00:15:00.490 --> 00:15:04.810
(Or do they have to be like, you know, the Fibonacci numbers or the prime numbers? Do they have to be like)

00:15:05.720 --> 00:15:11.050
(selected in some legitimate way?)
- No, you can really choose whatever crazy numbers you like.

00:15:11.060 --> 00:15:16.880
So you have to have infinitely many of them to possibly hope to get infinitely many approximations.

00:15:16.880 --> 00:15:21.060
(Brady: Ah, so you can't have a finite number of numbers in that denominator category?)

00:15:21.080 --> 00:15:24.820
No, you so you need to have an infinite number to really stand a chance

00:15:25.880 --> 00:15:27.580
because it's all asking:

00:15:27.580 --> 00:15:34.179
can you approximate numbers to, as good accuracy as you like. So if we in our approximations for pi

00:15:34.700 --> 00:15:36.700
you just stop with numbers

00:15:36.920 --> 00:15:40.750
less than 100 then well, you wouldn't know what the next decimal place was

00:15:40.750 --> 00:15:43.989
and so you couldn't tell the difference between pi in a whole load of other numbers.

00:15:44.750 --> 00:15:46.400
So you need to have infinitely many numbers,

00:15:46.400 --> 00:15:50.139
but the whole point of the theorem is it's a completely arbitrary

00:15:50.480 --> 00:15:55.150
collection of numbers and you can choose whatever you like. You don't have to choose it in some nice,

00:15:55.820 --> 00:16:01.960
mathematically sensible way. You can just pick, keep on picking numbers out of a hat in some crazy fashion and

00:16:02.780 --> 00:16:04.210
regardless of how you choose them,

00:16:04.210 --> 00:16:10.580
there's a simple way of telling whether you get almost all numbers being well approximated or almost no numbers being well approximated.

00:16:10.580 --> 00:16:13.760
(Brady: Was this a burning conjecture in mathematics?)

00:16:13.760 --> 00:16:16.480
So I think it was viewed as one of the

00:16:17.120 --> 00:16:21.340
more important problems in metric Diophantine approximation. So

00:16:22.010 --> 00:16:27.429
metric Diophantine approximation is when you're asking for these almost all results, and this was somehow the

00:16:28.520 --> 00:16:30.520
canonical example of a

00:16:31.160 --> 00:16:36.849
conjecture, and if you can't hope to do this, you can't hope to say more subtle things about how you can approximate numbers.

00:16:36.850 --> 00:16:42.900
(Brady: Give me a feel for what the proof was like. Was it, sort of a, one of these simple elegant ones or was it a bit of a monster?)

00:16:42.970 --> 00:16:45.370
So I think the basic idea is quite simple

00:16:45.370 --> 00:16:50.020
but then there's a whole load of annoying technicalities that meant that it turned into a bit more of a monster.

00:16:50.090 --> 00:16:56.289
But one of the fun things is that we use some ideas that weren't standard in the field and so, in particular,

00:16:56.810 --> 00:17:01.750
one way of trying to understand this situation is that we ended up drawing lots of graphs.

00:17:01.970 --> 00:17:09.310
So we'd just draw lots of dots where dots represent denominators and then we would draw lines between dots

00:17:09.500 --> 00:17:13.089
when there were mathematical complications to do with them. And

00:17:13.790 --> 00:17:20.319
then we would stare at these graphs and by understanding these graphs very well, we were able to understand the original problem.

00:17:20.319 --> 00:17:22.809
So one of the main things that we did was to

00:17:23.510 --> 00:17:26.020
try and understand the problem using these

00:17:26.600 --> 00:17:32.640
very simple ideas of dots and lines which is way to somehow visualise some of the complexities of what's going on.

00:17:33.040 --> 00:17:36.540
(Brady: Do you remember the moment that you and your collaborator had cracked it?)

00:17:36.540 --> 00:17:42.000
(Like was there a minute of the day where it was like - hey, I think we've done it! - or was it more gradual than that?)

00:17:42.370 --> 00:17:45.059
Okay, so there was one moment when I thought okay

00:17:45.700 --> 00:17:50.760
I think we've done it. There was a lot of work after that to make sure that actually all the fine details worked out in

00:17:50.760 --> 00:17:54.179
the way that I hoped, but yeah, there was definitely one moment where I was like, hang on a moment

00:17:54.179 --> 00:18:00.809
I think we can actually do this. That there was one issue that we've been stuck on for quite a little while and then suddenly

00:18:01.330 --> 00:18:03.480
we came up with a way of getting around that and

00:18:04.210 --> 00:18:09.480
yeah, that was pretty fun moment. So I was actually just sitting in this room thinking about the problem -

00:18:10.360 --> 00:18:13.709
it was a bit strange because I'd expected this to be a

00:18:13.870 --> 00:18:19.109
really tough battle that I was gonna have to grapple with for quite a long time and I really wasn't sure if I was gonna

00:18:19.150 --> 00:18:24.030
win the fight, but then suddenly I - there was a trick that actually managed to get around the problem.

00:18:24.429 --> 00:18:29.879
So rather than having to take on the battle that I thought I was going to have to fight head-on,

00:18:29.880 --> 00:18:32.339
I could just sidestep the whole thing and

00:18:32.890 --> 00:18:38.009
so, in some ways it felt a bit like a cheat that I had proven the theorem that I wanted to prove, but the

00:18:38.320 --> 00:18:42.360
key difficulty in the proof - actually, I didn't have to fight head-on at all.

00:18:42.429 --> 00:18:48.119
We were both pretty happy as soon as we got to this stage where we are pretty sure that everything would work out then.

00:18:48.640 --> 00:18:52.439
Although actually, I was at this thing - when I prove these

00:18:53.080 --> 00:18:56.850
results I get this initial joy that I'm very happy, but I haven't proved it,

00:18:56.850 --> 00:19:01.620
but I also get this fear that I'm about to completely embarrass myself in front of the mathematical community

00:19:02.020 --> 00:19:08.009
by claiming to have proved some well-known result, when actually there'll be some obvious mistake because I've - I

00:19:08.470 --> 00:19:10.450
put a plus instead of a minus somewhere.

00:19:10.450 --> 00:19:14.069
So you get this euphoric joy and adrenaline rush

00:19:14.070 --> 00:19:20.189
but then it's followed pretty quickly by complete fear that I'm about to trash my reputation and do something completely idiotic.

00:19:20.590 --> 00:19:26.100
(Brady: How often when you have those breakthrough moments like that do you end up finding you have made a mistake?)

00:19:26.300 --> 00:19:31.679
(And, and you just sort of keep quiet, and how often do you convert them into "Wow, I was right"?)

00:19:32.380 --> 00:19:35.579
Okay, there's sort of two different levels of breakthrough moments.

00:19:35.580 --> 00:19:40.559
There's ones where I don't really believe it myself and there's the ones that I really believe it myself.

00:19:41.549 --> 00:19:47.579
So it's very common in the day for you - for me to be like hang on this seems to be proving this amazing result,

00:19:47.580 --> 00:19:52.500
but I'm always a bit skeptical as to whether I've really done it. And those ones

00:19:52.960 --> 00:19:59.069
99%at the time, they turn out to be complete rubbish and there'll be some really stupid thing that I've messed up and

00:19:59.590 --> 00:20:03.569
you get up the next day, you look at what I've done and I was thinking 'how did I miss this?'

00:20:03.570 --> 00:20:05.570
I've been a complete idiot here.

00:20:05.679 --> 00:20:09.269
But then there's the other ones where you somehow intuitively

00:20:10.179 --> 00:20:16.199
feel that, yeah, this feels right. You know, I feel like I've got to grips with the problem and I've really solved it and

00:20:16.990 --> 00:20:21.480
okay, touch wood, fortunately so far all of those have turned out to actually work out.

00:20:21.480 --> 00:20:23.969
(Brady: And just lastly, when you have a breakthrough moment like)

00:20:23.970 --> 00:20:26.880
(that, like you have that idea the way to circumvent the problem and that.)

00:20:27.460 --> 00:20:33.200
(Is it such a big thing that you couldn't forget it or do you have to write it down quickly in case you forget the idea? Like - )

00:20:33.850 --> 00:20:40.079
Okay, I think I couldn't forget these things at all. That, normally when I've been working on a problem, I'm pretty obsessed by it

00:20:40.080 --> 00:20:43.770
and so it's sort of coursing through my body the whole time and in the back of my mind

00:20:43.770 --> 00:20:44.980
even when I'm

00:20:44.980 --> 00:20:49.110
having lunch and having the shower and things. And so I really have

00:20:49.270 --> 00:20:52.979
all the bits of the problem in my head and as soon as I have the idea for the problem,

00:20:52.990 --> 00:20:57.299
I'm not going to forget how easily. I often want to write it down to check that

00:20:57.299 --> 00:20:59.849
it really does work out the way that I think it works out.

00:21:00.070 --> 00:21:03.539
But yeah, it'll be over my dead body that I'm forgetting these ideas, I think.

00:21:06.030 --> 00:21:13.220
[Preview of more videos] Most recent time where we've seen someone really write down the conjecture officially was just over a hundred years ago by de Polignac,

00:21:13.260 --> 00:21:18.350
but he actually conjectured a stronger, more general form of the twin prime conjecture that's a bit more complicated.

